      "problem": "Given that $L\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\omega t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{\\omega }^{2}},$ find the Laplace transform $L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The first shifting theorem states that if\n$L\\{f(t)\\}=F(s),$\nthen\n$L\\{{e}^{at}f(t)\\}=F(s-a).$\n\nUsing the given result, we have\n\n$$\nL\\{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\}=\\frac{s}{{s}^{2}+{6}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the first shifting theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L\\{{e}^{-3t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)\\} & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{s+3}{(s+3)^{2}+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
